{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the story we like to tell about the \nbeginning of the universe. Space is expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8240,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly everywhere, but if you rewind that \nexpansion you find that all of space was once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13560,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compacted in an infinitesimal point of infinite \ndensity—the singularity at the beginning of time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expansion of the universe from this point \nis called the Big Bang. We like to tell this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "story because it's the correct conclusion from \nthe description of an expanding universe that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "followed Einstein's general theory of relativity \nback in the 1910's. But since then we've learned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37320,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so much more. Does our modern \nunderstanding of the universe still insist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42640,
    "dDurationMs": 6683,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a point-like Big Bang? Recent work actually \ngives us a way to avoid the beginning of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to understanding whether the universe had \na beginning is to decide whether there exists what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60440,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call a past singularity—whether all points \nin space were at the same point at time t=0.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And from the earliest models of the expanding \nuniverse, the answer seemed yes. When people like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75760,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alexander Friedmann and Georges Lemaitre solved \nthe equations of Einstein’s general relativity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80920,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back in 19-teens and twenties, they found that, \nif you rewind the expansion indeed all points  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88400,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converged on one point at the start of time. \nBut these guys made some big assumptions—for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example that the universe is perfectly smooth \neverywhere. It’s not—it’s at least a little  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lumpy or we wouldn’t have galaxies. Could \nthis non-perfect-smoothness prevent our rewound  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe converging on a single point? We’ll \ncome back to that—it’ll be important later."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that the universe is mostly perfectly \nsmooth hints at something else that may change  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117880,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our prediction of a beginning of time. To explain \nthe general sameness of our modern vast universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we needed to add something called cosmic inflation \nto stretch out tiny smooth patches in the early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe into a smooth giant universe. At first \ninflation was described as a period of extreme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exponential expansion that lasted for a tiny \nfraction of a second after the Big Bang. But then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141880,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we realized that this inflationary period didn’t \nhave to stop everywhere—perhaps it stops in spots,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creating bubble universes like ours, in which \ncase there’d be a greater inflating spacetime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that continues growing everywhere. But if this \n“eternal inflation” lasts forever into the future,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160320,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could it last forever into the past? If \nso, perhaps the universe had no beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166040,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, our description of the universe has \ngotten a little complicated over the past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169480,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100 years. We’re going to need to carefully \ntrack our path backwards in time to see if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we find a beginning. So we choose a \ncoordinate system for space and time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we trace those coordinates into the past \nthey and see if they reach a point where we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cannot trace them any further. This can happen \nif some aspect of the coordinate system “blows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191080,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up”--becomes infinite. We call such points \nsingularities. In standard big bang cosmology  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s a singulartiy in the past where all paths \nin space overlap and can’t be traced further. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203600,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is interpreted as the time before which there \nwas no space. But there’s at least one example  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210120,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you’ve heard of where a singularity \ndoesn’t mean the end of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215280,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Consider the black hole. The simplest type of \nblack hole is described by the Schwarzschild  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219960,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric. If we trace the Schwarzschild coordinates \nfrom outside the black hole in, we find they blow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up in two places. One is expected—at the center \nof the black hole we find a proper curvature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity—infinite spacetime warping. But \nthere’s also a singularity at the event horizon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There, it appears that the coordinate of time \nblows up. It’s as though we can’t trace time past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246160,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that horizon. We might be tempted to interpret \nthis as an uncrossable boundary, but we’d be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252200,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wrong. Just because Schwarzschild coordinates \ncan’t cross this boundary doesn’t mean space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257040,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and time end there. Sorry, you don’t bounce off \nthe event horizon, you fall straight through it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263320,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We only need a simple-ish \ncoordinate shift to create  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266480,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a map across the event horizon \nthat is free from infinities—from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularities. For example, Eddington-Finklestein \ncoordinates combine space and time in a way that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277240,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allows this continuous mapping all the way down to \nthe central singularity—where we do find an infinity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283920,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that does not go away. The event horizon is what \nwe call a coordinate singularity—it's similar to how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spherical coordinates have a singularity at \nthe poles where lines of latitude converge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that spacetime is extendable \nbeyond the event horizon because a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinate shift reveals the space beyond. \nIn fact, if we switch to a Penrose diagram  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we reveal new spaces that look like a mirror \nuniverse and a past white hole—these are the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313680,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fully extended coordinates… because we can, in \nprinciple, trace light rays to these places."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the central singularity of the black \nhole remains a physical singularity. No  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324120,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light ray can be traced through this \nto the other side. It’s reasonable to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328640,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state that spacetime ends at the \ncenter of a black hole. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332000,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no coordinate shift that extends our \nmap beyond the central singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what about the beginning of the \nuniverse? Is there a singularity there,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340000,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if so is it a real physical end-of-spacetime \nsingularity AKA the beginning of the universe? Or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345960,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is it a coordinate singularity like the event \nhorizon, with something on the other side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353120,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To figure this out, we need another tool.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s something we’ve discussed \nbefore: geodesic incompleteness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361080,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, a geodesic is the shortest \npath through spacetime in general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity. It describes the curved \npath travelled in a gravitational field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the straight path traveled out of one. \nLight travels something called a null geodesic,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374560,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is a path on which time itself \ndoes not pass for the traveller."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geodesics are normally thought of as \ncontinuing forever into the past and future,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384920,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that you can keep tracing these spacetime \nlines even beyond the segment travelled by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle. But sometimes a geodesic will reach a \ndead end where you can’t trace it any further.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are points of geodesic incompleteness, \nand they are interpreted as the literal end of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime—like the end of the map. The singularity \nat the center of a black hole is an example of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such a point. Geodesic incompleteness is how Roger \nPenrose proved that black holes contain physical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414240,
    "dDurationMs": 5844,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularities with his black hole singularity \ntheorem—something we have of course covered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420084,
    "dDurationMs": 5916,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geodesic incompleteness by itself is pretty \nconvincing, but we’re always left with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nagging worry that we plotted our geodesics in \nthe wrong coordinate system, like with the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432000,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole event horizon. Another sure sign of the \nend of spacetime in the black hole singularity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the curvature blows up— the strength \nof the gravitational field becomes infinite.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we can find such a curvature singularity in a \nway that’s independent of the coordinate system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451280,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can pretty much guarantee that the fabric \nof spacetime meets a bad end at that point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457560,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so now that we have some sharper tools \nto pry open the beginning of the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462640,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s see what we can find."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465040,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, for geodesic incompleteness the \nanswer appears to be that the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "did have a beginning. All geodesics—from \nthe arc followed by a thrown ball to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s orbit around the Sun—can be \nextended back in time and wind up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479600,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the same infinitesimal point. And that’s even \ntrue if you include cosmic inflation—even eternal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation. Demonstrating this past geodesic \nincompleteness has typically required certain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assumptions. For example, many arguments \nhave depended on the weak energy condition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is a sort of add-on to the main equation \nof general relativity that says you can’t have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative mass densities. But things probably get \npretty weird when you approach the past singularity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509040,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so it would be nice if we didn’t even \nhave to depend on this particular condition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514200,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More recently in 2003, Borde, Guth and Vilenkin \nmade a stronger argument for geodesic past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incompleteness that didn’t require an energy \ncondition. Instead, they only needed to assume  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528440,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the average expansion rate was always \npositive. This led to the Borde–Guth–Vilenkin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(BGV) theorem, which states that any universe \nthat has, on average, been expanding throughout  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541360,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its history can’t have been expanding \nforever—it must have a past boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, great. But can we be sure that this \npast boundary is really an end … or a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beginning to spacetime? What if \nspacetime is really extendible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like it was through the black hole event horizon? \nLet’s look at the other criteria that we used for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565120,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes—whether curvature becomes infinite. \nIf so then our universe surely must contain a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571800,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical singularity in its past. If it \ndoesn’t contain curvature singularity then this doesn’t guarantee that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can extend our spacetime, but we’d need to \ndo a bit more investigating to be sure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is what Geshnizjani, Ling, and \nQuintin do in their paper published  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590800,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last year. They applied a curvature \ntest to the beginnings of a range  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595720,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of different universes to check \nthe nature of any singularities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600560,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The results depend on the expansion \nhistory of the universe. Remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605320,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the BGV theorem tells us that \nuniverses that have, on average,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only expanded over their history must have \na past boundary, which might be interpreted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a beginning. This new study finds that \nuniverses with different expansion histories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t have to begin with a singularity, and \nso, perhaps, don’t have to begin. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a universe that expanded after a prior phase \nof contraction or from a prior static phase  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631400,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t have to have past singularities. At the \nsame time these expansion histories also seem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to violate certain energy conditions of general \nrelativity, and so may not be possible at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641360,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t really an entirely new \nfinding—more a confirmation that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you need to break some aspect \nof general relativity to avoid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a beginning to the universe. Otherwise \nthey mostly agree with the BGV theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Universe that have only ever expanded, do have a beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657640,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the researchers do find one unusual case \nin which the BGV theorem may be challenged.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that an inflating universe has \nexponential expansion. A pure exponential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670960,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "function with no vertical offset looks like \nthis: it grows rapidly into the future,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675160,
    "dDurationMs": 9720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also plateaus approaching but never reaching \nzero size in the past. The BGV theorem tells us that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even this ever-expanding universe should \nsuffer geodesic incompleteness into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past. There should be some sort of past boundary \nthat we’re tempted to identify as the beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That boundary can be depicted \nas the lower diagonal edges on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700600,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Penrose diagram. These represent past boundaries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703800,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe if there’s no way to \nextend a null geodesic beyond them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except, as it turns out there is a way. \nThis new study found that there’s a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713800,
    "dDurationMs": 6469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "type of spacetime that could extend to \nthis region beyond the past boundary.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720269,
    "dDurationMs": 2691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe is pretty well \ndescribed with something called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722960,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric. \nIt’s that perfectly smooth, flat, expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe that I mentioned earlier this episode. \nIf you add exponential expansion to that—whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734200,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as cosmic inflation or as dark energy—then \nFLRW metric can be considered to be a subset  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of something called de Sitter space. In fact, as \nthese researchers showed, it’s possible to think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747200,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our universe—our patch of FLRW space—as \njust a segment of a larger de Sitter space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754640,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The details of this are a bit much \nfor now, but long-story-short:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the BGV theorem insists that our universe \nhas a past boundary. If there’s no way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763680,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "map a null geodesic across that boundary then we \nshould interpret the boundary as a beginning of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time. But the new work shows there may be a \nplace for those null geodesics to travel to,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775960,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or have traveled from—the larger de Sitter \nspace that extends beyond our FLRW universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If our universe is extendable in this way, \nthat might just turn the past boundary of our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789720,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe into a coordinate singularity rather than \na real, physical one with infinite curvature etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this extended spacetime may be - probably is - as illusory as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "white hole and mirror universe of the \nPenrose diagram. But it’s intriguing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809680,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we have here a case where it’s not \njust blatantly obvious that the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814280,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rewinds into a non-traversable curvature \nsingularity at least in this one case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820480,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A word of caution though. In order to make \nthis work, our section of the universe—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825160,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FLRW section—has to transition smoothly \ninto the de Sitter space. The only way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831520,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for it to do that is for the exponentially \naccelerating component—often described as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836920,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmological constant—to dominate over any \ndensity fluctuations at the beginning of “our”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843360,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe. If those density fluctuations are \nvery small then spacetime is extendable past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850440,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past boundary of our universe and we \nmay not have a physical past boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But any significant density fluctuations would \nactually close off the boundary and should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn this transition into a hard curvature \nsingularity—eliminating the possibility that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s anything “before” it. And our universe \ndefinitely had density fluctuations at very,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874040,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very early times—otherwise we would \nnot have galaxies and planets and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877800,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "YouTubers today. It’s interesting that \nthe same lumpiness that Friedman et al  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glossed over when they predicted the big \nbang may make the big bang more likely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, that bodes poorly for an infinite past for \nthe universe. There probably was a beginning of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898880,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time. But the resolution of this is buried in the \nunknowns of inflationary cosmology. It also awaits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our theory of quantum gravity, because, even \nif the universe approaches a point of infinite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911640,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density in the past, our current understanding of \nphysics breaks down before we reach that point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most stunning thing about this sort \nof work isn’t even the answer—whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922720,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or not there was a beginning to \nthe universe. And by the way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the current answer is still probably yes. To me \nthe most incredible thing is that we can even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930440,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hope to answer questions that seem like they \nshould be far beyond our grasp. That through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937280,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pure reason we can learn one way or another \nwhether there was a beginning to spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS. \nBrilliant is where you learn by doing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with thousands of interactive lessons \nin physics, math, programming, and AI.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955160,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant helps build your critical thinking \nskills through problem solving—not memorizing. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961360,
    "dDurationMs": 6047,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each lesson is filled with hands-on problem \nsolving that let you actually play with concepts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967407,
    "dDurationMs": 9196,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you’re into vector mathematics, a fundamental concept for describing motion and orientation in space, Brilliant released a new course on Vectors. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976603,
    "dDurationMs": 8172,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this course you’ll learn the basics of vector operations, including scaling, transformations, polar coordinates, and the dot product. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984775,
    "dDurationMs": 5405,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can enhance your ability to visualize and solve complex problems in multidimensional spaces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990180,
    "dDurationMs": 10620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you’ll apply your newly developed skills to program a game, and design a logo to gain insights into how the physics of motion translate to a digital environment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000800,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To try everything Brilliant has to \noffer for free for a full 30 days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visit brilliant.org/spacetime or click on \nthe link in the description. If you go now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll also get 20% off an \nannual premium subscription."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015200,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. On September 26th, 1905 \nAlbert Einstein published his theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of special relativity and altered the course of \nphysics history. To celebrate the over 100 years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of relativity, we have two new items at the merch \nstore. First up is our universal speed limited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033680,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "embroidered patch. And next up is our 2D Light \nCone enamel pin with holofoil backing. With both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041400,
    "dDurationMs": 6794,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pin and the patch, you have everything you need to derive both special relativity all on your own in case you ever get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048194,
    "dDurationMs": 2498,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time traveled back to 1904. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050692,
    "dDurationMs": 5748,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This limited edition pre-sale will \nrun through October 7th and items will ship at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056440,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end of November. Just go to PBSspacetime.com/shop \nor click the link the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
